    "body": "Maine Pointe and dSilo are expanding their partnership to connect purchasing and supplier data with customer pricing and demand. The agreement builds on more than five years of work between the supply chain consulting firm and the AI company.\nWhat’s Related\nMaine Pointe plans to incorporate dSilo’s AI agents into the next version of the Buy sourcing module within its TVO Control Tower software . The companies say the integration will help teams identify potential improvements, decide which to pursue first, and track the work.\nThe expanded partnership aims to help companies understand how supplier costs and customer agreements affect profit margins and cash available for daily operations. It links purchasing decisions with sales data .\nAccording to the announcement, an earlier joint project processed more than 350,000 supplier invoices to examine pricing and purchasing volumes for individual items. The expansion will add AI agents intended to help teams act on those findings.\nChecking what companies actually pay\nThe purchasing work addresses a practical question: Do invoices match the prices and terms a company negotiated? On its procurement product page, dSilo says its agents compare contracted prices with actual payments and flag differences.\nThe software can also compare prices for the same item from different vendors and examine changes by location and time. Those comparisons are intended to give buyers more information for supplier negotiations and purchasing decisions.\nOther checks cover missed rebates, discounts and volume commitments. The company says its agents can compare contractual payment terms with actual payment dates, helping finance and purchasing teams investigate differences that affect cash.\nThe approach brings contract details together with transaction records. Teams can then check whether negotiated terms carried through to purchases and payments.\nMoving from findings to action\nOn dSilo’s larger agent platform, a new invoice, contract amendment or price change can trigger a series of tasks. Specialized AI agents extract information, match records, and check results.\nThe company says businesses can require human review and approval at defined points, including contractual and financial decisions. Reviewers receive the supporting evidence alongside the recommendation, and approved actions can be sent back to existing business systems.\nThat platform supplies the technology behind the planned Maine Pointe integration. The companies intend to use it to help purchasing teams move from identifying pricing and contract issues to deciding what to address and tracking the resulting work.",
